<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>This tab is for Admins and people with the CanManageFiles role.</FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="Uploading">
        <FluentText>
            Open the folder you want, then select <strong>Upload</strong>. Drop files onto the box or select it to
            choose them: up to 20 at a time, each up to 100 MB. Audio files (MP3, WAV, FLAC, OGG, AAC, M4A, WMA)
            are also added to the music library.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            You can upload images (PNG, JPG, GIF, WebP, BMP, SVG, HEIC), video (MP4, M4V, WebM, MOV, MKV, AVI),
            audio, PDFs, Word, Excel and PowerPoint files and their OpenDocument versions, RTF, text, CSV, JSON
            and ZIP files. Other types, such as programs or web pages, are refused.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            Two files in the same folder can't have the same name. If you upload one that's already there, the new
            copy gets a number, like <strong>poster (2).png</strong>.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Folders">
        <FluentText>
            <strong>New folder</strong> makes a folder inside the one that's open. Folder names must be different from
            the other folders beside them.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Renaming, moving and deleting">
        <FluentText>
            Every file and folder has a <strong>⋯</strong> button (or right-click it) with <strong>Rename</strong>,
            <strong>Move to…</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. The same buttons are in its details. Renaming a
            file keeps its type: you change the name before the extension.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            <strong>Move to…</strong> shows your folders. Pick one and select <strong>Move here</strong>. A folder
            can't be moved into itself or one of its own folders.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            With a file or folder selected, <strong>F2</strong> renames it, <strong>Delete</strong> deletes it and
            <strong>Space</strong> previews a file.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="What uses a file">
        <FluentText>
            A file's details list everything in Lanyard that uses it under <strong>Used by</strong>: the company logo,
            a projection program, a training course, party printout artwork, an onboarding email or a song in the
            music library. Files that are used somewhere have a link badge in the grid.
        </FluentText>
        <HelpNote Intent="MessageBarIntent.Info">
            Deleting a file that's in use asks you first. It keeps working where it's used while it's in the
            Recycle bin, and it can't be deleted for good until nothing uses it.
        </HelpNote>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
